<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Nazgul Ezhe, a 52-year-old widow from the Jalal-Abad region of Kyrgyzstan, left her home country five years ago in search of work to support her two children. Struggling with financial difficulties and limited opportunities, she faced numerous hardships, including language barriers, lack of proper registration, and ignorance of labor rights.)
/E (Nazgul Ezhe, a 52-year-old widow from the Jalal-Abad region of Kyrgyzstan, left her home country five years ago in search of work to support her two children. Struggling with financial difficulties and limited opportunities, she faced numerous hardships, including language barriers, lack of proper registration, and ignorance of labor rights.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
522 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Over the past year, Nazgul worked in various low-paying jobs, the most recent being in a fur processing workshop where she experienced forced labor. Without an employment contract, she endured long hours, low wages, and threats of violence. Her employer imposed fines for mistakes and withheld pay, pushing her into a cycle of debt. She worked more than 12 hours a day under constant fear of punishment, eventually losing her home due to unpaid rent and being left without money for food.)
/E (Over the past year, Nazgul worked in various low-paying jobs, the most recent being in a fur processing workshop where she experienced forced labor. Without an employment contract, she endured long hours, low wages, and threats of violence. Her employer imposed fines for mistakes and withheld pay, pushing her into a cycle of debt. She worked more than 12 hours a day under constant fear of punishment, eventually losing her home due to unpaid rent and being left without money for food.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (At her lowest point, while her son in Kyrgyzstan was hospitalized after a violent attack, Nazgul sought help from the Insan-Leilek Public Foundation. The foundation intervened, providing her with legal, psychological, and humanitarian support. They facilitated her safe return to Kyrgyzstan, where she could reunite with her son and begin her long journey of recovery and reintegration.)
/E (At her lowest point, while her son in Kyrgyzstan was hospitalized after a violent attack, Nazgul sought help from the Insan-Leilek Public Foundation. The foundation intervened, providing her with legal, psychological, and humanitarian support. They facilitated her safe return to Kyrgyzstan, where she could reunite with her son and begin her long journey of recovery and reintegration.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T ("My exploiter threatened me so much, that I was afraid to ask for help. I was convinced that she will find out and punish me again. I am so happy that I am finally home. I do not know how to express my gratitude to everyone who helped me get out of this bondage and return to my son" Nazgul shared, relieved to be free from her exploiters.)
/E ("My exploiter threatened me so much, that I was afraid to ask for help. I was convinced that she will find out and punish me again. I am so happy that I am finally home. I do not know how to express my gratitude to everyone who helped me get out of this bondage and return to my son" Nazgul shared, relieved to be free from her exploiters.)
/Alt ()
